Z||000008||mwbackus||11-23-1999||10:15 PM||||This desire for GM to bring back the Fiero is rather confusing to me.  Consider the following:<P>1)  A brand new model Fiero would probably cost well over $30k, a price that is very likely to be unattainable for the average Fiero owner.<P>2)   Consider the VW bug, the classic VW was a popular restoration platform, good examples were showing a marked appreciation in value.  The release of the new model VW essential reclassified these rare antiques into "older model cars in good condition".<P>3)  The Nissan 300zx has held its value way above what would be expected. It has even seen some appreciation in value since its demise.  I have no doubt that if a new model 300zx were released, the values of these older cars would plummet.<P>4)  A new model Fiero would not open up a source of "parts" for the older models.  The new model would incorporate design changes that would essentially prevent parts swapping.<P>5)  The Acura NSX is still in production, it's mid-engined design has everthing that a Fiero enthusiast could ever dream about.  The price is way below that of a comparable exotic, and although very few Fiero enthusiasts would consider spending the $85k needed to buy a new one, a "used" 91 Acura NSX in very good condition can be obtained for around $30k. <P>I buy Fiero's because they are inexpensive to purchase, which leaves a considerable amount of funds available to address the weaker points of the cars mechanicals and body.  I enjoy restoring these cars, and would have little interest in a new model.  I would like a better understanding of why one would see a new model Fiero as an advantage to the Fiero enthusiast, so any comments are, as always, appreciated.<P>Mike<BR>(Fiero details available in user profile)<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||COUNTIBLIS||11-24-1999||12:16 AM||||so let me get this straight, noone wants to see a new fiero?||||reg||5
Z||000010||Monkeyman||11-24-1999||12:54 AM||||First, just let me say that I LOVE Fieros, but...  I don't want to see a "new" one.  mwbackus spoke of the VW Beetle.  One thing he forgot to mention was that, other than the same basic shape, the "new" Beetle is NOTHING like the original.  What if Pontiac renews the Fieros' lease on life.  What would it be like?  Probably front-engine, front wheel drive, no space frame and like he said very expensive.  Besides, it might increase the cost of the originals.  I don't want to pay anymore for Fieros than I have to.  (I'm not cheap, just poor)  I don't think any of you want the Fiero to increase in value too much unless all you intend to do is buy and resell in which case you aren't a Fierolic, just a used car salesman.||||reg||1
Z||000011||WKDFIRO||11-24-1999||01:06 AM||||They say that you can never go home. GM will never be able to make a Fiero the way they did before. No matter what they do it will fall short of the original. I would love to see a new Fiero but GM's mindset would never allow it to get as close to a Corvette as the original. <P>Besides look at all of the other "classics" reincarnated by GM:<P>-Monte Carlo (What styling?)<BR>-Chevy Malibu (More vanilla styling lost in the crowd...)<BR>-Impala (Do I REALLY need to say anything?)<P>If this new Fiero is really coming down the pike do we really want it to be called a Fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000012||2M4 Dale||11-24-1999||01:58 AM||||Just a few years ago, 87-88, Pontiac was thinking of resurecting the GTO on the Grand AM body. While the Grand Am isn't a bad car it is also not a GTO. Being the overall Pontiac nut, that I am, I don't think bringing back the Fiero would be such a good thing. Pontiac is quite capable of designing new cars. Maybe they will come up with something new and totally awesome. Don't hold your breath...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Racer 1||11-24-1999||03:55 AM||||Does anyone remember the movie "The Blackhole" with the red robot maxamillion? That like GM theroy of car evolution. Fix it till its broke. I could just imagine a new 1999 Fiero 350 engine 16 inch rims supercharged . Damnit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> Damnit||||reg||9
Z||000014||ChadMan||11-24-1999||09:05 AM||||I'm ready for a new Fiero.  There's nothing wrong with the concept of building a sports car from existing passenger-car mechanical parts - some great sports cars were put together this way (Porsche 356, Austin-Healey Sprite, C1-C2 Corvette, etc.)<P>Our cars were put together largely from X-car parts, and we love them.  Imagine how great a Fiero could be put together from Grand Am mechanicals and an all-new space frame.  Offer the 150-hp 2.4 as a base motor, and the 3800 V6's (or even the new 3.5 DOHC V6) as an option.<P>There's no reason this car would be any more expensive to manufacture than a Saturn SC.  The base Fiero ought to start at less than $20K.||||reg||1
Z||000015||DJRice||11-24-1999||09:15 AM||||Anybody remember the Mid to Late 80's Nova's that popped up.  Theres another good reason to leave the Fiero name alone.<P>The design and production of the Fiero represented a deviation from the norm of how GM did things in the mid-80's.  It lost because of good old business/office politics and a misunderstanding of the Fiero Customers.  (Power steering, if added sooner, may well have turned the tide for the Fiero.  Just look at the MR2).<P>GM did go back and revist the lessons learned and products developed for Fiero.  The result of that is <B>SATURN</B>.  One has to wonder if there would have been a mid-engine Saturn, but nowadays, people want to be able to haul multiple people plus groceries and the kids soccer equipment, plus get 30MPG. (Unless they go buy a Ford Excursion) A mid engine design just isnt practical for more than 2 people.<P>You also have to look at the average persons opinion of a Fiero.  Thats another good reason for GM to leave the name alone.  Most people (who dont know better) will say "Those cars are Fire Hazard Death Traps."  I'm sure that every one of us has seen the look on a mechanics face when he sees a Fiero pull up.<BR>It seems they are almost afraid to work on them. Its not like they are any harder to work on than your average FWD vehicle by any car manufacturer.  And if anything, you dont have to worry about steering hardware interfering with engine repair.<P>As the saying goes "Be careful what you ask for, you may get it."  How bad would everyone feel if we did see a new Fiero and it was a Miata or Eclipse lookalike.  Especially since the Eclipse is a Mustang lookalike now.<P>We may have something to look forward to, however.  The Firebird is being discontinued indefinitely.  Maybe GM is looking to drop the good old fashioned 5.7L Powerplant for a 6 cyl. with a turbo or supercharger.  If one can make 300+HP with six, why worry with eight?  Its about time that C.E.R.V. concept hit the street anyway.<P>Wow, my fingers are tired now.||||reg||3
Z||000016||87GT||11-24-1999||09:24 PM||||I am registered to get all kinds of info on new Pontiac vehicles.  I recently got the new information on the 2000 Bonneville.  I got all kinds of litereature, pics, and even a video to watch!  Anyways, in one of the pamphlets, it shows the all new GTO.  This car has 20" Rear wheels!  It goes on to say that it will be rear wheel drive(thank god) and that it will be powered by some version of the LS1 350.  The last thing it said is that it has a great chance of making it to production.  Im going to miss the Firebird/TA, but I am very excited about the possible return of the legendary GTO! ||||reg||1
